ds of others he hadn't yet  been deported  but the parents of 506 children still  can't be found and only about one-third  of those parents  are thought to be living right here in  the u.s paz says there is another factor  complicating reunifications now  he says many parents feel their children  are better off in the u.s  without them if i go back to my country  i lose my life i lose the life  of my kids they're gonna disappear us  you're saying that these parents are so  scared  that that they would much rather be  separated i'll stay in my home country  and the kids stay in the u.s they keep  running for their lives in the countries  they keep running always for their lives  trying to be safe  paz believes he is one of the lucky ones  but he still  worries about the long-term effects on  his family  when they take my daughter from me they  give me another  person back they didn't give me back my  daughter  angeline was completely different  to give you some context this morning on  why so many of these families come here  to the u.s we took a  took a look at a study from doctors  without borders from 2018 that spoke  with migrants  from honduras el salvador and guatemala  two-thirds of the people they spoke with  said they had experienced either  kidnapping murder or the disappearance  of a relative  in those countries highlighting  obviously  how dire the situation is to them  gail maria thank you so much and dire is  the word guys we went to the border at  the time and the majority of the people  there  came because they were afraid and  they're looking for better lives nobody  in this room  would accept being separated from their  children no these young kids who were  separated from their children and we  the united states government did that  every time i think about that it makes  my blood boil don't make such a  dangerous journey no  unless what you're leaving is even yes  yeah were there some bad actors in the  crowd  probably but that was certainly not t